---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "Simple vs Compound Interest: What Is the Difference? - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How simple interest and compound interest are calculated, why they produce different totals over time, and where each is used.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/simple-vs-compound-interest.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Savings and loans</p>
    <h1>Simple vs Compound Interest: What Is the Difference?</h1>
    <p class="lead">Both methods charge or pay interest on money, but simple interest uses only the original amount, while compound interest also earns interest on interest already added.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Simple interest</h2>
<p>Interest = principal × rate × years. 1,000 at 5% for 3 years gives 150 in interest, for a total of 1,150.</p>
<h2>Compound interest</h2>
<p>Interest is added to the balance and then earns interest too. The same 1,000 at 5% compounded yearly for 3 years grows to about 1,157.63, a little more than simple interest.</p>
<h2>Why the gap grows</h2>
<p>The difference is small over a few years but widens over decades. It is why long-term savings grow faster with compounding, and why unpaid debt grows quickly too.</p>
<h2>Where each is used</h2>
<ul>
<li>Simple interest is common on some short-term loans and certain bonds.</li>
<li>Compound interest applies to most savings accounts and credit cards.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>Use the <a href="/tools/simple-interest-calculator.html">Simple Interest Calculator</a> for simple interest, and the <a href="/tools/compound-interest-calculator.html">Compound Interest Calculator</a> to compare.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
</Base>
